# cinterp @VERSION@ — @TARGET@ release tarball

`cinterp` compiles C ahead of time into a compact stack-bytecode package
(`.cib`) plus compile-time FFI call plans, then executes it in a small Zig VM
that calls native C functions directly. There is no libffi, no JIT and no
executable memory. This tarball is the **@TARGET@** build.

## What is in this tarball

| Path | Contents |
| --- | --- |
| `@RUNNER@` | runtime executable: `@RUNNER@ [options] package.cib [entry]` |
| `include/cinterp.h` | C embedding API (ABI 2) |
| `lib/` | shared library plus static/import library for @TARGET@ |
| `pkgconfig/cinterp.pc` | relocatable pkg-config metadata |
| `cmake/` | `cinterpConfig.cmake` and `cinterpConfigVersion.cmake` |
| `lib/cmake/cinterp/` | the same CMake package config, in the layout `find_package` searches |
| `rust/cinterp-sys/` | Rust FFI crate with a safe `Vm` wrapper and a runnable example |
| `docs/` | CIB format, embedding, ABI compatibility and C/IR subset documentation |
| `examples/` | C sources used by the release smoke tests |
| `debug/` | split debug files, when the build produced them |
| `README.md`, `LICENSE`, `VERSION`, `SBOM.spdx.json` | this file, license, version, SPDX SBOM |

<!-- compiler-only -->
**This tarball ships the compiler.** In addition to the runtime files above it
contains:

| Path | Contents |
| --- | --- |
| `bin/cinterp-cc` | the C-to-CIB compiler (invokes `zig cc` as its frontend at compile time) |
| `bin/cinterp` | wrapper script that execs `bin/cinterp-cc` |
| `THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md` | attribution for the derived external-symbol data described below |

Compile and run the bundled example:

```sh
./bin/cinterp compile examples/hello.c -o hello.cib
./@RUNNER@ hello.cib
# result: 42
```

`cinterp-cc compile app.c -o app.cib --target <target>` selects the package
ABI: `x86_64-linux` (this tarball), `x86_64-windows`, `i686-windows` or
`aarch64-linux`. Compile for the target whose runtime you will load the
package into. `--dump-target` / `--dump-imports` inspect the result, and
`--cc-path` points the frontend at a specific `zig` binary.
<!-- /compiler-only -->

<!-- runtime-only -->
**This is a runtime-only tarball.** It does **not** contain `bin/cinterp-cc`.
Compile packages with a compiler build for the same target: the Linux x86-64
release tarball ships one, or build the compiler from the source tree with Zig
0.13:

```sh
cd cinterp/compiler && zig build
./zig-out/bin/cinterp-cc compile --target x86_64-windows app.c -o app.cib
```

The rest of this file explains how to load a prebuilt `.cib`.
<!-- /runtime-only -->

## Release tarballs for @VERSION@

| Tarball | Contents |
| --- | --- |
| `cinterp-@VERSION@-sysv-x86_64` | **compiler + runtime**, Linux x86-64 (`bin/cinterp-cc`, `bin/cinterp`) |
| `cinterp-@VERSION@-win64-x86_64` | runtime only, Windows x64 |
| `cinterp-@VERSION@-win32-x86` | runtime only, Windows x86 |
| `cinterp-@VERSION@-arm64` | runtime only, Linux AArch64 |

Only the Linux x86-64 tarball contains `bin/cinterp-cc` and
`THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md`; the other three are runtime-only.

## Run a package

```sh
./@RUNNER@ hello.cib                    # run entry point "main"
./@RUNNER@ hello.cib main -- a b c      # explicit entry, C argv (argv[0] = "a")
./@RUNNER@ --lib ./libfoo.@LIB_EXT@ app.cib   # add an explicit native library handle
./@RUNNER@ --dump-target --dump-imports --load-only app.cib
./@RUNNER@ --version
```

`--lib PATH` registers an explicit `dlopen` (Unix) or `LoadLibraryA` (Windows)
handle before execution. Packages normally name their native providers in the
CIB link metadata (`CIPI`) and the runtime loads those libraries itself; use
`--lib` for libraries the metadata does not mention, or to force a different
build of one it does. Libraries are never unloaded — a handle stays valid
until the process exits.

Exit status is 0 on success, 1 for a load/run failure and 2 for a usage or I/O
error. `main`'s return value is printed as `result: N` and is available as the
VM result; it does not become the process exit code.

## C host through pkg-config

```sh
export PKG_CONFIG_PATH="$PWD/pkgconfig"
cc host.c $(pkg-config --cflags --libs cinterp) -o host
LD_LIBRARY_PATH="$PWD/lib${LD_LIBRARY_PATH:+:$LD_LIBRARY_PATH}" ./host app.cib
```

`cinterp.pc` is relocatable — `prefix` is derived from the location of the
`.pc` file, so the tarball can be unpacked anywhere. `Libs.private` carries the
extra system libraries needed for a static link (`-ldl -lpthread -lm` on Unix,
empty on Windows): use `pkg-config --static --cflags --libs cinterp` when
linking `libcinterp.a` (on Windows that is `cinterp_static.lib`). On Windows,
put `lib/` on `PATH` instead of setting `LD_LIBRARY_PATH`.

## CMake consumer

```cmake
cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.16)
project(host C)

find_package(cinterp 0.1 REQUIRED)

add_executable(host host.c)
target_link_libraries(host PRIVATE cinterp::cinterp)
```

Point CMake at the unpacked tarball:

```sh
cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH="$PWD"
cmake --build build
LD_LIBRARY_PATH="$PWD/lib${LD_LIBRARY_PATH:+:$LD_LIBRARY_PATH}" ./build/host app.cib
```

The package config is installed both at `cmake/` and at
`lib/cmake/cinterp/`; `-Dcinterp_DIR="$PWD/cmake"` works too if you prefer to
name it explicitly. `cinterpConfigVersion.cmake` accepts requests up to
@VERSION@ within the same major series, so `find_package(cinterp 0.1 REQUIRED)`
matches this tarball while `find_package(cinterp 1.0)` fails.

## Rust binding

`rust/cinterp-sys` contains the raw `ci_vm_*` bindings plus a small safe `Vm`
wrapper. It expects the native library in `../../lib` and the header in
`../../include` relative to the crate directory and links `-lcinterp` itself;
override those paths with `CINTERP_LIB_DIR` / `CINTERP_INCLUDE_DIR`.

```sh
cd rust/cinterp-sys
cargo run --offline --example run_hello -- /path/to/app.cib --expect 42
# result = 42
cargo test --offline
```

The example prints `result = <value>` and, with `--expect N`, exits non-zero
when the VM result differs. `Error::code` is the category reported by
`ci_vm_last_error_code()` (a `CI_E_*` value), `Error::status` is the negative
value the failing call returned, and `Error::message` is `ci_vm_last_error()`.
To depend on the crate from another project:

```toml
[dependencies]
cinterp-sys = { path = "vendor/cinterp-sys" }
```

One `Vm` is single-threaded. Use separate `Vm` instances for independent
execution; callback trampolines are process-wide and retired when their owning
VM is destroyed.

## Documentation

- `docs/cib.md` — CIB package format, link metadata, status codes and tooling
- `docs/embedding.md` — C embedding API and host integration
- `docs/compatibility.md` — ABI, SONAME and versioning policy
- `docs/ir-subset.md`, `docs/ir-parser-differences.md`, `docs/zig-frontend.md` — accepted C/IR subset
- `rust/cinterp-sys/README.md` — Rust crate

